      "id": 1,
      "text": "South Rockwood was founded in 1863 by John Strong, who named the village after Rockwood, Ontario.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Rockwood%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Strong opened the South Rockwood post office on July 5, 1877, marking the town’s first federal service.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Rockwood%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The village officially incorporated in 1955, becoming a self-governing community within Monroe County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Rockwood%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Huron River traces the northern edge of South Rockwood, forming the county line with Wayne County’s city of Rockwood.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Rockwood%2C_Michigan",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Monroe, the county seat, lies just 13 miles southwest of South Rockwood, a short drive for residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_Rockwood%2C_Michigan",
